---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "What to Check Before Buying a Used Treadmill (2026): Full Checklist";
const description =
  "Run it for ten minutes with your weight on it, lift the belt, and put a hand on the motor hood. Fifteen checks that separate a bargain from someone else's dead controller board.";

const CHECKS = [
  {
    check: "Deck surface under the belt",
    good: "Slick, smooth, no groove along the running line",
    bad: "Dry, glazed or grooved means deck and belt together.",
  },
  {
    check: "Belt underside and edges",
    good: "Matte texture, edges intact",
    bad: "Polished, cracked or frayed means it has run dry.",
  },
  {
    check: "Motor hood after ten minutes",
    good: "Warm to the touch",
    bad: "Too hot to rest a hand on. Walk away.",
  },
  {
    check: "Belt under your full weight",
    good: "Holds speed with no hesitation",
    bad: "Sagging under load points at friction or a tired motor.",
  },
  {
    check: "Incline through its full range",
    good: "Smooth, reaches maximum, no grinding",
    bad: "An incline motor is an expensive repair.",
  },
  {
    check: "Console, all buttons and programmes",
    good: "Every button responds, no error codes",
    bad: "Dead segments and dropouts point at the board.",
  },
  {
    check: "Hours on the meter",
    good: "Under 500 hours on a home machine",
    bad: "Over 1,500 hours is a machine near mid-life.",
  },
  {
    check: "Frame, welds and folding hinge",
    good: "No flex, no rust, latch engages positively",
    bad: "A twisted frame cannot be adjusted straight.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Buying Guides", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-of/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Used Treadmill Checklist", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What should I check before buying a used treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Run it for at least ten minutes with your full weight on it, then check the motor hood temperature, lift the belt to inspect the deck underneath, run the incline through its full range, and press every console button. Those five cover the expensive failures. Never buy a treadmill you have not seen running.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How many hours is too many on a used treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On a home machine, under 500 hours is low use and over 1,500 is approaching mid-life. Commercial-grade machines are built for far more and 3,000 hours on one is not alarming. Hours matter less than how the machine was maintained — 400 hours on a deck that was never lubricated is worse than 1,200 on one that was.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is buying a used treadmill worth it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It can be excellent value, because treadmills depreciate steeply and many are barely used. The risk is concentrated in two parts — the motor and the controller board — which are expensive and hard to assess without running the machine. A well-maintained mid-range machine at half price is a good buy; an untested one at any price is a gamble.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What are the red flags when buying a used treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A seller who will not let you run it, a machine that is unplugged or dismantled when you arrive, a hot motor hood after a short test, a burning smell, error codes on the console, a belt that sags under weight, and any rust on the frame or deck. Each of these points at a repair costing a substantial share of the price.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much should a used treadmill cost?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "As a rough guide, expect to pay 40 to 60 per cent of the current new price for a well-maintained machine two to four years old, and considerably less beyond five years. Factor in what it will cost you to move it — a full-size treadmill is 200 to 350 lb and rarely fits in a car.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Which used treadmills are worth buying?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Machines from brands that still sell parts, with a continuous horsepower rating suited to your weight and pace, and a deck long enough for your stride. Commercial-grade machines from gyms are often good value because they are built for far heavier use than a home puts on them, though they are heavy and may want a dedicated circuit.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-09-06",
      dateModified: "2026-09-06",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-09-06",
    modifiedTime: "2026-09-06",
    author: "Morgan Reyes",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "used treadmill",
      "second hand treadmill",
      "treadmill buying checklist",
      "treadmill inspection",
      "home gym",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-used" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-used)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated September 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          What to Check Before Buying a <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Used Treadmill</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          Treadmills depreciate steeply and many are barely used, so the bargains
          are real. So are the machines being sold because the controller board is
          about to go. Twenty minutes of inspection tells you which one you are
          looking at.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#checklist" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            The Checklist
          </a>
          <a href="#red-flags" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Walk-Away Signs
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">September 6, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="5.9k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-red-900">Never buy a treadmill you have not seen
          running.</strong> A seller who says it is unplugged, dismantled, or in
          storage is asking you to accept the two most expensive failure modes on
          trust. If it cannot be demonstrated under your own weight for ten minutes,
          walk away regardless of the price.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#before-you-go" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Before You Go</a>
          <a href="#checklist" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. The Inspection Checklist</a>
          <a href="#ten-minute" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. The Ten-Minute Run Test</a>
          <a href="#deck" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Lift the Belt</a>
          <a href="#hours" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Reading the Hour Meter</a>
          <a href="#red-flags" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Walk-Away Signs</a>
          <a href="#price" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. What to Pay</a>
          <a href="#logistics" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Getting It Home</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The used treadmill market is unusually good for buyers, because a large
          share of home treadmills are bought in January and abandoned by March. It
          also contains a steady supply of machines whose owners have just been
          quoted for a controller board. The two look identical in a photograph and
          take about twenty minutes to tell apart in person.
        </p>

        <div id="before-you-go" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Before You Go</h2>
          <p>
            Three things settled from home save wasted journeys, and one of them
            eliminates most listings outright.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Find the exact model and check the specification.</strong> Ask
            for the model number from the sticker on the frame. Then confirm the
            continuous horsepower rating suits your weight and pace and the deck is
            long enough for your stride — a bargain on a machine that is the wrong
            size is not a bargain. The sizing is in our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            and the
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check that parts are still available.</strong> Search for a
            replacement belt, deck and console for that model. If nothing comes up,
            the machine is unrepairable at any price, and the first fault ends it.
            Mainstream brands are safe here; obscure direct-to-consumer brands from
            five years ago frequently are not.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Confirm it will fit and can get in.</strong> Measure the room
            including the 6 feet of clearance behind, measure the ceiling, and
            measure the narrowest point of the route into the house. This is the
            single most common regret with a used purchase, because there is no
            returning it. The numbers are in
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">dimensions and space requirements</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Then ask the seller one question before travelling: will it be plugged in
            and running when I arrive? A no is a complete answer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="checklist" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Inspection Checklist</h2>
          <p>
            Eight checks, in the order to do them. Take a phone torch and be willing
            to spend twenty minutes.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-8 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[720px] w-full text-sm border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-gray-900 text-white">
            <tr>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Check</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">What Good Looks Like</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">What It Means If Not</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              CHECKS.map((row, i) => (
                <tr class={i % 2 === 0 ? "bg-white" : "bg-gray-50"}>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900 align-top">{row.check}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">{row.good}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-500 align-top">{row.bad}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Weight the results by cost of repair. A worn belt is a consumable and a
          reasonable basis for negotiating rather than walking. A hot motor hood or
          an incline that grinds is a repair worth a large share of the machine's
          price, and those are refusals rather than discounts.
        </p>

        <div id="ten-minute" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. The Ten-Minute Run Test</h2>
          <p>
            The most informative thing you can do, and the reason a seller who will
            not permit it has told you what you need to know.
          </p>
          <p>
            Get on the machine with your own weight — not the seller's, and not a
            polite two-minute walk. Start at walking pace, then bring it up through
            the speed range to something close to what you would actually use, and
            keep it there for the better part of ten minutes.
          </p>
          <p>
            While you are on it, feel for a belt that <strong>hesitates under your
            footstrike</strong>. That is friction or slack, and although it is often
            fixable with lubrication it can also be a tired motor. It is the same
            diagnostic ambiguity described in
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-slows-down-when-i-step-on-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt slows down when I step on it</a>,
            and on a used machine you have no history to resolve it with.
          </p>
          <p>
            Listen. A steady hum is fine. A squeal or rumble that rises with belt
            speed is a roller bearing; a rhythmic tick once per rotation is the belt
            seam; a creak under your weight is loose hardware. The
            <a href="/treadmill-squeaking-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">noise guide</a>
            separates them, and only the last is trivial.
          </p>
          <p>
            Then, at the end of the ten minutes, put a hand on the motor hood. Warm is
            normal and expected. Hot enough that you do not want to leave your hand
            there means the motor is working far harder than it should, and on a
            machine you have owned for ten minutes that is not a problem you want to
            inherit. Any smell of hot electronics ends the visit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Lift the Belt</h2>
          <p>
            The check that reveals how the machine was treated, and almost nobody
            does it. Ask the seller to switch off and unplug, then reach under the
            belt at the centre of the deck.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Feel the deck surface.</strong> It should be slightly slick, like
            a waxed board, and leave a faint film on your finger. Dry, dusty or
            grippy means it has not been lubricated — and since that is the single
            most consequential maintenance item, it tells you how the rest was
            treated. A grey paste means over-lubrication and dust, which is less
            serious but still informative.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look for a groove.</strong> Shine a torch along the deck. A
            visible channel or a polished stripe where the running line has been is
            deck wear, and a new belt on a worn deck does not last. Many decks are
            reversible, which softens this, but it turns a belt purchase into a belt
            and a strip-down.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check the belt itself.</strong> The underside should feel matte
            and slightly textured. Polished or glazed means it has run dry for a long
            time. Then run a hand along both edges — fraying or hardening means it
            has been rubbing the frame, which points at a machine that has been
            running off-centre, possibly because the frame is twisted.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do the lift test.</strong> Grip the belt at the middle of the deck
            and pull up. Two to three inches is correct. Much less means it has been
            over-tightened, which loads the roller bearings and stretches the belt,
            and the damage from that is already done. The standard is set out in
            <a href="/how-tight-should-treadmill-belt-be/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how tight a treadmill belt should be</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hours" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Reading the Hour Meter</h2>
          <p>
            Most machines track cumulative hours and total distance somewhere in the
            console diagnostics or settings menu. Ask the seller to show you, or look
            it up for that model beforehand.
          </p>
          <p>
            As rough bands for a home machine: under 500 hours is genuinely light
            use, 500 to 1,500 is a normally used machine, and above 1,500 the belt,
            deck and rollers are all at or past mid-life. Commercial-grade machines
            are built to a different standard entirely and 3,000 hours on one is not
            alarming.
          </p>
          <p>
            The important caveat is that <strong>hours are less informative than
            maintenance</strong>. Four hundred hours on a deck that was never
            lubricated is a worse machine than twelve hundred hours on one that was
            serviced quarterly, because the damage from friction is cumulative and
            not visible in the number. This is why the deck check in the previous
            section carries more weight than the meter does.
          </p>
          <p>
            A large distance with modest hours means the machine was used for running
            rather than walking, which is harder on everything. Very low hours on an
            older machine are usually genuine — the January purchase abandoned in
            March is the archetypal good buy — but check for rust and perished
            components, because sitting unused in a damp garage has its own costs.
          </p>
          <p>
            If there is no hour meter, ask directly how often it was used and whether
            it was ever lubricated. The answer to the second question, and how
            confidently it comes, is worth more than the answer to the first. The
            interval that should have been followed is in
            <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how often to lubricate a treadmill</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="red-flags" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Walk-Away Signs</h2>
          <p>
            Some findings are negotiating points. These are not.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It is not plugged in when you arrive.</strong> Dismantled, in
            storage, in a garage with no outlet, or the seller has just moved it —
            whatever the reason, you cannot test what matters. This is the most common
            way people buy a dead controller board.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A hot motor hood or any burning smell.</strong> The motor and
            controller are the expensive parts, they fail from heat, and both symptoms
            say the machine is already fighting something. A replacement board can
            cost a third to a half of the machine's value fitted, which is the
            arithmetic in
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long treadmills last</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Error codes, dropouts, or a console that behaves oddly.</strong>
            A machine that cuts out during your test will cut out during your
            training, and console and board faults are model-specific repairs that are
            rarely cheap.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Rust anywhere on the frame or deck hardware.</strong> It means
            damp storage, and damp is bad for electronics in ways you will not see
            until later. Surface rust on a bolt is survivable; rust on the frame or
            around the motor is not.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A frame that flexes or a folding latch that will not engage
            positively.</strong> A twisted frame cannot be adjusted straight, and it
            steers the belt off-centre permanently. A folding machine that has been
            dropped carries exactly this fault, and it is why a belt that will not
            stay centred is worth investigating rather than dismissing — see
            <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to centre a treadmill belt</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. What to Pay</h2>
          <p>
            Treadmills depreciate faster than almost any large household item,
            largely because moving them is difficult and because the market knows
            most of them are barely used.
          </p>
          <p>
            As a working guide: 40 to 60 per cent of the current new price for a
            well-maintained machine two to four years old, and appreciably less
            beyond five years. Very low-hour machines from the first year or two hold
            more value, and that is often fair — a nearly-new machine at 60 per cent
            is a better buy than a tired one at 30.
          </p>
          <p>
            Price in what you will have to do. A belt is a modest sum; a belt and a
            deck flip together is a job as well as a cost. Budget for a bottle of
            silicone and an immediate lubrication regardless, because you will not
            know when it was last done and it is the cheapest insurance available.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Gym clear-outs</strong> deserve a mention. Commercial machines
            sold when a facility refits are frequently good value, because they are
            engineered for a duty cycle a home will never approach and even a
            high-hours example may have plenty left. The trade-offs are weight —
            often 350 lb and rarely folding — and that some want a dedicated circuit.
            Our
            <a href="/commercial-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">commercial treadmill guide</a>
            covers what to expect from them.
          </p>
          <p>
            Do compare against new. Entry-level new machines with a warranty exist at
            prices that overlap with mid-range used ones, and a warranty is worth
            real money on a category whose expensive failures are electrical. The
            current new-market picture is in
            <a href="/treadmill-buying-guide-2026/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">the complete buying guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="logistics" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Getting It Home</h2>
          <p>
            The part people cost at zero, and it regularly turns a good deal into an
            expensive afternoon.
          </p>
          <p>
            A full-size treadmill weighs 200 to 350 lb, is awkwardly balanced, and
            does not fit in a car. Plan for a van and at least two people, and be
            aware that carrying one down stairs is genuinely hazardous — the weight
            is concentrated at the motor end and it does not stay where you put it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Ask whether it can be partly dismantled. On many machines the console
            uprights unbolt, which drops the width substantially and makes doorways
            and stair turns manageable. Do that at the seller's house rather than
            discovering the need at yours, and photograph the wiring at each
            connector before you unplug anything.
          </p>
          <p>
            Transport it upright or on its side as the manual specifies, and secure
            it — a treadmill sliding in a van damages the console and the frame
            alignment, and frame alignment is the thing you cannot fix afterwards.
          </p>
          <p>
            Once home, do three things before the first session: level it properly
            with a spirit level across the frame, lubricate the deck, and check the
            belt tension and tracking. Twenty minutes of setup on a machine of unknown
            history is the difference between a good purchase and one that starts
            developing faults in month two. The full routine is in the
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance schedule</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I check before buying a used treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Run it for at least ten minutes with your full weight on it, then
                check the motor hood temperature, lift the belt to inspect the deck
                underneath, run the incline through its full range, and press every
                console button. Those five cover the expensive failures. Never buy a
                treadmill you have not seen running.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many hours is too many on a used treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a home machine, under 500 hours is low use and over 1,500 is
                approaching mid-life. Commercial-grade machines are built for far more
                and 3,000 hours on one is not alarming. Hours matter less than how the
                machine was maintained — 400 hours on a deck that was never lubricated
                is worse than 1,200 on one that was.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is buying a used treadmill worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be excellent value, because treadmills depreciate steeply and
                many are barely used. The risk is concentrated in two parts — the
                motor and the controller board — which are expensive and hard to
                assess without running the machine. A well-maintained mid-range
                machine at half price is a good buy; an untested one at any price is a
                gamble.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are the red flags when buying a used treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A seller who will not let you run it, a machine that is unplugged or
                dismantled when you arrive, a hot motor hood after a short test, a
                burning smell, error codes on the console, a belt that sags under
                weight, and any rust on the frame or deck. Each of these points at a
                repair costing a substantial share of the price.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much should a used treadmill cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                As a rough guide, expect to pay 40 to 60 per cent of the current new
                price for a well-maintained machine two to four years old, and
                considerably less beyond five years. Factor in what it will cost you
                to move it — a full-size treadmill is 200 to 350 lb and rarely fits in
                a car.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which used treadmills are worth buying?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Machines from brands that still sell parts, with a continuous
                horsepower rating suited to your weight and pace, and a deck long
                enough for your stride. Commercial-grade machines from gyms are often
                good value because they are built for far heavier use than a home puts
                on them, though they are heavy and may want a dedicated circuit.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying Used</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these has bought somebody a machine that needed a repair costing more than the purchase.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Accepting a short, polite test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Two minutes at walking pace proves nothing. Thermal faults, sagging under load and cut-outs all appear after five to ten minutes at a real speed with your own weight on the belt. Ask for ten minutes and use them, and treat any reluctance as an answer in itself.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never lifting the belt</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The deck underneath is where a machine's maintenance history is written. A dry, grooved or glazed deck means a belt and a deck flip together, and it tells you what the rest of the servicing was like. Thirty seconds with a torch and a finger is the most informative check available.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not checking that parts still exist</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Search for a belt, a deck and a console for that exact model before travelling. If nothing is available, the machine is unrepairable and the first fault ends it, however good it looks today. Obscure direct-to-consumer brands from a few years ago are the usual casualties here.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Costing the transport at zero</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A full-size treadmill is 200 to 350 lb, does not fit in a car, and is genuinely hazardous to carry downstairs. Budget for a van and two people, ask whether the uprights unbolt, and photograph the connectors before dismantling anything. A frame damaged in transit is the one fault you cannot adjust out.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

